  <!-- ========== SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="strategic-recommendations">
    <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Actionable intelligence synthesized from the data analysis. Every recommendation is anchored to specific findings.</p>

    <div class="rec-grid">
      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>1. Source Apple Watches from the UK</h4>
        <p>The UK is the single best sourcing market for Apple watches, with a 28.2% discount to global median ($395 vs. $550). With 215 active listings, volume is sufficient for sustained procurement. Resell into Australia ($792, +44%), Japan ($688, +25%), or the U.S. ($550) for immediate margin.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 3 geographic analysis — UK Apple median $155 below global</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>2. Build a Japan → UK Garmin Pipeline</h4>
        <p>Japan offers Garmin at $506 median (10.7% below global) with 161 listings predominantly offering worldwide shipping. The UK values Garmin at $775 (+36.7% above global). This $269 per-unit spread, combined with Japan's shipping infrastructure, makes this the most operationally ready arbitrage play.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 3 — Japan Garmin 161 listings, global shipping; UK Garmin $775 median</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>3. Target Samsung UK Demand</h4>
        <p>Samsung's UK premium is the largest in the dataset: +55.7% above global median ($825 vs. $530). Source from the U.S. ($500), Japan ($437), or Malaysia ($478). Even after shipping and fees, the $347–$388 gross margin per unit is compelling.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 3 — Samsung UK outlier premium, multiple low-cost source markets</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>4. Partner with Brand-Specialist Power Sellers</h4>
        <p>The top 10 sellers control 29.8% of the market, and most specialize in a single brand. Rather than competing with them, partner for supply access. Seller_0001 (65 Garmin listings from Japan, worldwide shipping) and Seller_0035 (136 Apple open-box listings) are prime partnership targets.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 5 — power seller brand specialization pattern, 48.2% market concentration</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>5. Prioritize Garmin for Predictable Margins</h4>
        <p>Garmin's tight price distribution (σ = $198 vs. Apple's $834) means procurement risk is lowest. Combined with zero new-vs-used premium, you can source used Garmin at essentially the same price as new. The resale price band of $506–$890 across geographies provides margin certainty.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 2 — Garmin lowest σ; -0.3% condition premium; Section 3 — $384 geographic spread</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>6. Invest in Condition Grading Only for Apple</h4>
        <p>Apple is the only brand where condition materially impacts price (+17.7% new vs. used). For Apple inventory, invest in professional testing, cleaning, and condition grading to capture the premium. For Garmin and Samsung, skip the expense — condition doesn't move the price needle.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 2 — Apple 17.7% condition premium vs. near-zero for Garmin/Samsung</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>7. Don't Overweight Case Size in Pricing</h4>
        <p>Case size explains almost none of the price variance (r = 0.021). Model tier — not physical dimensions — drives price. Focus inventory databases on model identification (Ultra, Fenix 8, Galaxy Watch Ultra) rather than case size categories.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 4 — overall r=0.021, brand-level correlations all below 0.11</div>
      </div>

      <div class="rec-card">
        <h4>8. Enable Worldwide Shipping Capabilities</h4>
        <p>Listings with worldwide shipping carry a $146 median premium ($672 vs. $526). This is partly selection bias (power sellers list premium products), but it also reflects access to higher-price markets. Building worldwide shipping capability is a prerequisite for executing geographic arbitrage strategies.</p>
        <div class="rec-data">Based on: Section 5 — $146 worldwide shipping premium; 97% from power sellers</div>
      </div>
    </div>

